{
 "id": "2022-jo-waterfall",
 "name": "Waterfall Image Impressions",
 "description": "Method replication: the two photographs are the paper's own stimuli, shown on a participant's screen instead of an 85-inch high-resolution display in a climate chamber. A waterfall image (Tatsusawa Fudo Falls, Fukushima Prefecture) and an urban control image (Eitai Street, Otemachi, Tokyo). Participants see the two images in counterbalanced order (randomly waterfall first or urban first), as in the paper's crossover design: for each image a grey screen for 60 s, the image for 90 s, then the paper's modified semantic differential, three pairs of contrasting adjectives on a 13-point scale (comfortable - uncomfortable, relaxed - awakening, natural - artificial; labels very, moderately, slightly, indifferent). Age and gender are asked at the end. Rebuilt from Jo, Ikei & Miyazaki (2022), Physiological and Psychological Responses of Viewing a Waterfall Image: A Crossover Study, International Journal of Environmental Research and Public Health 20(1): 565, with the images cropped from its Figure 2 (trimmed at the top to remove the panel letters) and the scale from its Methods and Figure 5 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: waterfall, urban; order records which came first. Analysis as in the paper: paired comparison (Wilcoxon signed-rank) of the waterfall and urban images on each adjective pair; the paper (27 women in their 20s) found the waterfall image rated slightly to moderately comfortable, slightly to moderately relaxed and moderately to very natural, against an indifferent, slightly awakening and moderately artificial urban image (all p < 0.001). Differences from the original study: (1) online, without the heart rate variability, heart rate and near-infrared time-resolved spectroscopy measures, and on an ordinary screen instead of a large display viewed from 110 cm; (2) the Profile of Mood States 2nd Edition is not included because its items are not printed; (3) no 5-10 min break between the two images; (4) the paper prints the scale's labelled points (very, moderately, slightly, indifferent) but not the wording of the instructions, which is ours, as are the stored values (-6 to +6, positive = comfortable / relaxed / natural); (5) the age and gender questions are ours; the paper's sample was female university students aged 21-29.",
